WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.000
A deviation table is your compass’s report card, heading by heading.

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.450
Look at this one as it builds.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.725
On zero zero zero, two degrees east.

4
00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:13.525
On zero three zero, one east.

5
00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:16.000
On zero six zero, none.

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.900
On zero nine zero, two west.

7
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.650
On one two zero, three west.

8
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.500
And on one five zero, two west.

9
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.725
See how it swings smoothly from east to west?

10
00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:29.600
That’s normal.

11
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.225
Now, what if your heading falls between two lines?

12
00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:35.250
You interpolate.

13
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.775
Say you’re steering zero seven five magnetic.

14
00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:45.500
That’s halfway between zero six zero, with no deviation, and zero nine zero, with two west.

15
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.825
So what’s the deviation?

16
00:00:48.825 --> 00:00:50.750
About one degree west.

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.700
One caution.

18
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:58.100
Some tables are entered with the compass heading, and some with the magnetic heading.

19
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.250
Read the top of the column before you use it.
